Part 1.

    .          Any character except new line
    ^          Beginning of line
    $          End of line
    A          Matches 'A'
    a          Matches 'a'
    \d         Is a digit
    \D         Is not a digit
    \w         is a word character
    [A-E]      Matches any uppercase letter from A-E
    [^A-E]     Does not match any uppercase letter from A-E
    X?         There are 0 or 1 instances of 'X'
    X*         There are 0 or more instances of 'X'
    X+         There are 1 or more instances of 'X'
    X{n}       There are n instances of 'X'
    X{n,m}     There are between n and m instances of 'X'
    (abc|def)+ There are 1 or more instances of 'abc' or 'def'
    abc(def)   Matches "abcdef"

    [[:alpha:]]   Matches alphabetical characters
    [[:alnum:]]   Matches alphanumeric characters
    [[:blank:]]   Matches blank characters
    [[:digit:]]   Matches digits
    [[:lower:]]   Matches lowercase letters
    [[:print:]]   Matches printable characters
    [[:punct:]]   Matches punctuation
    [[:space:]]   Matches whitespace
    [[:upper:]]   Matches uppercase letters
    [[:cntrl:]]   Matches control characters
    [[:graph:]]   Matches visible characters (no whitespace)
    [[:xdigit:]]  Hexadecimal digits

    1. $ ls -l | grep ^-
        This command checks if '-' appears at the beginning of 
        any line in the output from the command  ls -l  and 
        prints the line.  This results in showing the 'regular
        files' in the current directory.
    2. $ ls -l | grep ^d 
        This command checks if 'd' appears at the beginning of
        any line in the output from the command  ls -l  and 
        prints the line.  This results in showing all directories
        in the current directory.
    3. $ ls -l | grep js$
        This command checks if each line from the output of 
        the command  ls -l  ends in js and prints those lines.
        This results in showing the javascript files in the
        current directory.
    4. $ ls -l | grep 'so*t'
        This command checks if each line from the output of 
        the command  ls -l  begins with 's', has 0 or more 
        instances of 'o', and ends in 't' and prints those
        lines.
    5. $ grep '^$' filename
        This command checks if each line from the file 
        'filename' matches with the beginning of a line
        immediately followed by the end of a line and prints
        these lines.  This results in locating blank lines
        in 'filename' and printing them.

    1. $ echo "Test with this" | awk'/[^i]th/{print $0}'
        Test with this
    2. $ echo "Test with this" | awk'/[^i]th/{print $0}'
        Test with this


Part 2. 

    1.
        IPV4: $ grep -E "^([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}$" ip.txt 
        | grep -E -v "(((25[6-9])|(2[6-9][0-9])|([3-9][0-9][0-9])
        |(\b0[0-9]{1,2}\b)))" > output_p1.txt

        IPV6: $ grep -E "^([[:alnum:]]{1,4}:){7}[0-9]{1,4}$" ip.txt > output_p1.txt

    2.
        $ grep -oEi '\w+' words.txt | sort | uniq -ci | sort -nr 
        | awk '{print $2, $1}' | grep -Ei -v '\b(the|a|an|is)\b' > output_p2.txt 
